A dilute gas is relatively easy to compress because compression mainly reduces the average distance between molecules rather than making the molecules themselves smaller (Figure $7$).
This uses the approximation that molecular size is much smaller than molecular spacing;
at higher densities, size and intermolecular interactions can no longer be neglected.

The source also describes his work on atmospheric electricity and the origin of Earth's magnetic field.
These statements preserve the textbook's historical attribution;
labels such as "creator" and "first" are not presented as independently verified, exclusive historical conclusions.

Table salt (Figure $11$), diamond (Figure $12$), and magnesium (Figure $13$) are the source's examples of crystal lattices;
the particles are ions in salt and atoms in diamond and magnesium.
The ordered internal arrangement can produce regular geometric faces under suitable growth conditions (Figure $14$), but not every piece of a crystal has a complete regular outer shape.

